  "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{sec}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3{csc}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{-3{csc}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t}{2{sec}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t} \\\\  & =-3{csc}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,{csc}^{3}⁡t{cot}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{3}⁡t})(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}){cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{{cos}^{4}⁡t}{{sin}^{5}⁡t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t{cot}^{4}⁡t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){cot}^{4}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\, \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
